From: NFCX maintainers
Subject: [PATCH] enumerate Windows UART ports through DOS device mappings
Upstream-Status: Pending
Reference: https://github.com/nfc-tools/libnfc/blob/libnfc-1.8.0/contrib/win32/libnfc/buses/uart.c#L243-L283
Reference: https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/win32/api/winbase/nf-winbase-querydosdevicea

The libnfc 1.8.0 Windows UART backend treats GetDefaultCommConfig success as
proof that a COM port exists. Some USB-to-serial devices can be opened through
their COM name but do not pass that configuration lookup, so pn532_uart
autoscan never tries the port. An explicit pn532_uart:COMx connstring still
works in that situation.

Use QueryDosDeviceA to test whether each COM name has a current Windows device
mapping. The later pn532_uart scan still opens every candidate exclusively and
performs the PN532 communication check before reporting it.

diff --git a/contrib/win32/libnfc/buses/uart.c b/contrib/win32/libnfc/buses/uart.c
--- a/contrib/win32/libnfc/buses/uart.c
+++ b/contrib/win32/libnfc/buses/uart.c
@@ -242,15 +242,11 @@ uart_send(serial_port sp, const uint8_t *pbtTx, const size_t szTx, int timeout)
 
 BOOL is_port_available(int nPort)
 {
-  TCHAR szPort[15];
-  COMMCONFIG cc;
-  DWORD dwCCSize;
+  char szPort[15];
+  char targetPath[1024];
 
   sprintf(szPort, "COM%d", nPort);
-
-  // Check if this port is available
-  dwCCSize = sizeof(cc);
-  return GetDefaultCommConfig(szPort, &cc, &dwCCSize);
+  return QueryDosDeviceA(szPort, targetPath, sizeof(targetPath)) != 0;
 }
 
 // Path to the serial port is OS-dependant.
